The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Disorderly conduct means conduct which shall be deemed to be offensive and adverse to public safety and thereby classified as disorderly conduct. The term "disorderly conduct" is exhibited or occurs when any person:
(1) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another person whereby any person is placed in reasonable fear of safety of his life, limb or health;
(2) Acts in a violent or tumultuous manner toward another person whereby the property of any person is placed in danger of being destroyed or damaged;
(3) Endangers the lawful pursuits of another person by acts of violence, angry threats and abusive conduct;
(4) Causes, provokes or engages in any fight, brawl or riotous conduct so as to endanger the life, limb, health or property of another person;
(5) Assembles or congregates with another person for the purpose of causing, provoking or engaging in any fight or brawl;
(6) Jostles or roughly crowds or pushes any person in any public place;
(7) Collects in bodies or in crowds for unlawful purposes as defined by the current ordinances of the county;
(8) Frequents any public place with intent to obtain money from other persons by illegal and fraudulent schemes, tricks, artifices or devices;
(9) Assembles, for the purpose of engaging in any fraudulent scheme, device or trick, to obtain any valuable thing in any place or from any person in the county, or who shall aid or abet therein;
(10) Accosts or attempts to force his company upon any person;
(11) Utters, while in a state of anger in the presence of another, any obscene, vulgar or profane words or epithets;
(12) Who is knowingly present at any place where illegal gaming or illegal sale or possession of alcoholic beverages or narcotics or dangerous drugs is practiced, allowed or tolerated;
(13) Acts in a dangerous manner towards others;
(14) Uses fighting words, as defined by state law, directed towards any person who becomes outraged and thus creates a turmoil;
(15) Assembles or congregates for the purpose of trouncing upon another;
(16) By acts of violence, interferes with another's pursuit of a lawful occupation;
(17) Congregates with another in or on any public way so as to halt the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic and refuses to clear any such public way when ordered by the law enforcement officers or other lawful authority;
(18) Enters upon the tracks, rights-of-way, yards, piggyback facilities, agencies, building or other properties of any railroad company operating in the county, provided that this subsection shall not apply to on-duty officers, agents or employees of any railroad company operating in the county, or those persons conducting bona fide business with the railroad company;
(19) Tampers with any railroad switch, wire or line or signal or places any object on the track that would otherwise obstruct the movement or operation of a train, provided that this subsection shall not apply to on-duty officers, agents or employees of any railroad company operating in the county or those persons conducting bona fide business with the railroad company;
(20) Gets on or off any locomotive engine, railroad car, trailer or container, provided that this subsection shall not apply to on-duty officers, agents or employees of any railroad company operating in the county or those persons conducting bona fide business with the railroad company.
(Code 2005, § 30-33; Ord. of 8-5-1991, § 3)

